en:
Thou art not here by chance, but hither cast;
The door is barr’d, and barr’d shall be to last,
Till thou dost cry the word all mortals cry
When walls close in and none will make reply.
Four tales lie sleeping in this chamber’s keep:
A life upon a dial, wound full deep;
A witness in the glass; a feast of dread,
Where one chair waiteth for the risen dead;
Two houses that will never meet in light.
Each tale, once play’d, shall yield a letter right;
Count thou the marks beneath, to learn its place.

All the world’s a stage,
And one man in his time plays many parts,
His acts being seven ages, drawn in brass:
The Cradle first, wherein the infant cries;
The Satchel next, ’neath schoolboy’s sullen eyes;
The Rose, for lover sighing at the moon;
The Sword, the soldier’s quarrel, sudden, soon;
The Scales, the justice weighing right from wrong;
The Cane, the old man’s staff, his walk not long;
The Candle snuff’d, last scene of all, when light
Is gone, and second childhood brings the night.
Begin beneath the arrow wrought in gold,
And follow where it points, as time is told.

The crownèd host upon the second seat;
His hatted fellow, one chair right, doth meet;
The guest upon the fourth. The first stands bare,
For one unbidden, whom none sees, sits there.
Count thou the notches carvèd on each back,
From left to right, as runs the arrow’s track.

One crown of tin, much dented by the fall;
One skull of plaster, grinning at the wall;
Two lanterns lacking lenses, dark and cold;
A gown for her who came not, as foretold;
One ghost, that will not learn his part, nor leave.

Tonight: the Lovers at the Window.
Let Romeo, who kneels, a Rose extend;
Fair Juliet the sealèd Letter tend;
Good Friar Laurence hold the Vial deep,
The potion that doth counterfeit a sleep.
The Skull and Crown to other plays belong;
Leave them upon the cart, or all goes wrong.

Beware the ides of March!
I cried it once to Caesar in the street;
He heeded not, and fell at Pompey’s feet.

The Kalends is the first of every moon;
The Ides of March, of May, of July’s noon,
And of October fall upon fifteen;
All other months upon thirteen, I ween.

From London’s fog to Reading art thou led;
Here Dyer’s promise offered board and bed.
For pay she took the infants into care;
Yet murder hid behind that promise fair.
In eighteen ninety-six her crimes were tried;
The court found murder where her words had lied.
These empty cots remember trust betrayed;
The house below is memory remade.

Six figures burn; their lesser sparks ignore.
Count but the linked stars watched by keepers four.
No pair may claim one figure of the night;
Their sum is three-and-twenty points of light.
Sun, Tide look forth; Moon, Ash look o’er the shoulder;
Turn each until its sight doth meet its holder.
The brass beneath records which way they face;
Press thou the seal to judge all four in place.

The Sun hath two more stars than Tide can see;
The Ash hath one more star than Moon’s decree.
Tide counts the fewest of the chosen four;
No watcher shares the heaven of one more.
The sum upon the seal binds every light;
Their faces may deceive thee in the night.

When all four sights are true, let footsteps speak;
The marks upon their backs are what ye seek.
From fewest stars to most, the keepers read;
Their back-marks, not their heavens, guide thy tread.
Four stones in order wake the buried door;
Step clear between, and wait for naught before.
A stranger stone unthreads thy steps alone;
The watchers keep the truth already known.

Three points a Triangle in heaven make;
Four shape the Diamond where the pale lights wake.
Five crown the sky; six turn the Key of night;
Seven frame the Eye that keeps its inward light.
Eight weave the Serpent where the heavens bend;
The lesser, lonely stars no count shall lend.

In eighteen sixty-three the trains were run
Beneath the streets, away from London’s sun.
On January’s tenth the public came;
The Metropolitan bore forth its name.
From Paddington to Farringdon they rode;
With steam and gas the buried passage glowed.
The first such railway bore the city through;
An older year now opens unto you.

The union leaks: shut feed ere bolts are drawn;
Make fast the joint, then let the feed run on.
Each furnace mark gives steam two, return one;
Relief takes one from each till work is done.
Let steam show THREE, return show ONE to thee;
Then loose the brake and set the carriage free.
Beside the cab the green control shall wait;
Board thou the train, and leave this sleeping gate.
